In the original xv6 util version, `kalloc` calls **`panic("kalloc")`** when `kmem.freelist` is empty.

```c
char*
kalloc(void)
{
  struct kmem *c = &kmem;
  char *v;

  acquire(&c->lock);
  v = c->freelist;
  if(v == 0)
    panic("kalloc");       // ← dies here, never returns
  c->freelist = *(char**)v;
  release(&c->lock);
  return v;
}
```

The kernel simply crashes — there is no graceful failure path. Later versions (e.g., xv6-riscv) changed this to return `0` (NULL) so callers can handle allocation failure.

